Title: Nick and Me
Credit: Written by
Author: Unknown
Source: Adapted from book
Draft date: 8/13/2026
Notes: Medium = photoreal_live_action; contemporary Midwest family drama, naturalistic faces, fabric weave, and available light

FADE IN:

EXT. KIRK STREET - DAY

A five-story brick walk-up hunches over a Milwaukee sidewalk. Fire-escape stairs stripe the facade. A dented grocery cart rattles past a stoop.

(SOUND: bus air-brakes hiss two blocks over, a distant train horn)

THE NARRATOR, late twenties, lean working-class build, short brown hair, worn jacket and jeans, hauls two plastic grocery bags toward the door. Behind him, NICK, early thirties, tall and thick as a wrecking ball, reddish-brown hair uncombed, a scar on his chin and another over his left eyebrow, puzzle-piece face held together by old brawls, carries six bags like they weigh nothing.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Big brother was mine. Not that stupid book from junior high. My real Big Brother.

Nick shoulders the street door without breaking stride. People on the sidewalk notice him and step aside.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick was the guy everybody was scared of. He had a pair on him the size of steel wrecking balls.

INT. KIRK STREET STAIRWELL - DAY

Five flights of scuffed linoleum and iron railings. Nick takes them two at a time. The Narrator lags, bags cutting his fingers.

(SOUND: boots pounding stairs, a baby crying behind a door)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
We lived downtown on Kirk Street after dad took off when I was about eight. Fifth-floor walk-up. A real pain in the ass.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

A cramped fifth-floor railroad flat. Brown overstuffed chair, rabbit-ear television, a blue afghan, a line of pill bottles on the kitchen counter. MA, sixties, dull brown hair, tired brown eyes, janitor-stooped shoulders, cardigan over a housedress, sits in the chair with the television glow on her face.

Nick dumps the bags on the counter. The Narrator sets his down and rubs his hands.

(SOUND: game-show applause leaking from the television)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Luckily, when I went to the store with Nick, he could load up about six bags and take them up in one trip.

Nick opens the fridge, checks the milk, nods once at Ma, and is already turning for the door.

INT. MOXIE'S GYM - DAY

A corner gym that smells of iron and liniment. Free weights, a curl bench, a man mid-set with a forty-pound bar. Nick walks in, reddish-brown hair still uncombed, scars catching the fluorescent light. The Narrator trails him.

(SOUND: plates clanging, a jump-rope slapping rubber)

The man on the bench sees Nick coming, drops the bar, and offers the bench with both hands. Nick nods, swaps the bar for a fifty-pound one, and starts curling like it is a warm-up.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I'm not too shabby, but I ain't gonna curl no fifty pounds as a warm-up. That's okay. Nick is my brother.

Nick finishes a set and looks over. The Narrator pretends to stretch so he will not have to take the bar.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I know he'll stand up for me no matter what.

EXT. KIRK STREET ALLEY - DAY

Dented garbage-can lids mark bases in a brick alley. A scuffed kickball sits in a puddle. The present alley is empty. Wind pushes a loose lid.

(SOUND: lid scraping asphalt, a dog barking behind a fence)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Milwaukee is where we lived all our lives. We used to play kickball back here.

A memory hangs in the bricks: a ball-shaped hole imagined in a kitchen window, a furious old woman, a twelve-year-old Nick stepping forward.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Little Mark Conner kicked a ball through old Mrs. Engel's kitchen. Nick said he was the one who did it.

The Narrator stands where home plate used to be.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ma paid for the window. A week later Nick gave her sixty bucks. He never told her it was Mark. None of us did.

EXT. KIRK STREET - DUSK

Nick's 1980 Pontiac Trans Am idles at the curb, burnt orange with yellow flames, T-top, the big eagle still on the hood. Exhaust splitters tick as they cool. People at the light turn their heads.

(SOUND: big V8 rumbling, a car stereo thumping bass)

The Narrator runs a hand along the flame job. Nick, thick and scarred, grins around a cigarette.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He got a great deal from some guy in Chicago. Twelve-grand. Seventy-five thousand miles. I love riding in that thing.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Afternoon light through dirty windows. Ma's bedroom door is shut. Nick, reddish-brown hair wild, heavy boots, stands in the living room like he owns the air. The Narrator hovers by the kitchen.

(SOUND: refrigerator hum, a faucet dripping)

NICK
What's Ma doin' back in the bedroom at this time of day?

THE NARRATOR
I don't know, why?

NICK
You got to keep an eye on her. She shouldn't be sleeping in the middle of the day like that.

Nick jabs a thick finger toward the shut door.

NICK (CONT'D)
That's how old people go downhill. They start sleepin' all day.

THE NARRATOR
I'll do a better job. I'll make her sit out here with me.

NICK
That's more like it.

Nick leaves. The Narrator looks at the shut door, then at the television already tuned to a game show Ma is not watching.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
All she wants to do is watch game shows and cooking channels. She never even bothers to call her sister.

He sits on the arm of her chair.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Ma worked years as a janitor. Those little kids are pigs. She started having back problems and had to take retirement.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Macaroni in a pot. A pizza box from the Narrator's job. Ma eats slowly. The Narrator watches her miss her mouth once and recover.

(SOUND: fork on a cheap plate, the building's pipes knocking)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Between her and me with my pizza job money, we make ends meet. It's not always pretty.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Nick is back, filling the kitchen doorway. The Narrator washes a pan.

(SOUND: water hitting a metal sink)

NICK
You don't need a degree to get a good job. All you need is to know somebody.

THE NARRATOR
Then talk to your boss. Get me on at the metal factory.

Nick's mouth flattens. He does not answer that.

NICK
What kind of shit do you want to take at school anyway?

THE NARRATOR
I don't know. Something real.

NICK
All that crap is just a bunch of nerds talking to hear themselves talk.

Nick leans on the counter, scars catching the light.

NICK (CONT'D)
The real world is here. You're not gonna be a doctor or lawyer. Hell, you're too old anyway.

The Narrator stares at the dishwater.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick is right. I am too old. I haven't got the brains. Besides, I have to take care of Ma.

He dries his hands.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I do like reading though. I don't tell nobody that.

EXT. PIZZA PARLOR SIDEWALK - DAY

A storefront pizza place, glass door, a crack in the cement. The Narrator walks his bike to the rack, late-twenties, lean, pizza-shop polo under the jacket. A girl coming the other way catches her toe on the cement and falls into his shoulder.

SIONNA, mid-twenties, slim, pale heart-shaped face, short brown hair, light-blue eyes like sky before sunrise, black cheap V-neck, jeans, blue tennis shoes, a fish-symbol necklace, a big backpack.

(SOUND: bike chain ticking, a car horn, her shoe scuffing concrete)

THE NARRATOR
Are you okay?

SIONNA
Yes.

She looks down and brushes off her shirt. They look at each other and do not know what to say.

THE NARRATOR
I work here. I'm just going to work. What is your name?

SIONNA
Sionna.

THE NARRATOR
Sionna? That's different. I never heard of that. What does that mean?

She starts to tell him about an Irish goddess and the River Shannon. He is not really listening. He is looking at her eyes.

THE NARRATOR
You want to come in and have some pizza?

SIONNA
No thanks, I don't eat pizza.

She says it like cool blue, like no big deal.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
But if you want to go have a cup of coffee sometime, you can call me.

He takes her number with fingers that do not quite work. She walks away and looks back over her shoulder, as if trying to remember what he will look like tomorrow.

(SOUND: her footsteps fading on cement)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
No girl has ever been interested in me. She was not cute. She was pretty. Pale, like an old English story.

He watches her move, smooth, like there are no bones in her body.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I knew her. I would have remembered somebody like her. Intense. Yea, that's it.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Same afternoon. Pill bottles lined on the counter in a straight row. Ma, cardigan, tired brown eyes now glassy and empty, rummages, mumbling. The Narrator comes in still thinking about Sionna.

(SOUND: bottles clicking together, Ma's slippers shuffling)

THE NARRATOR
Ma, what's the matter?

MA
I can't find my pain pills. I know I put them here somewhere.

She turns, lost.

MA (CONT'D)
Did we go to church today? Did you see Mrs. Canasta there?

THE NARRATOR
Ma, what are you talking about? We don't go to church anymore.

He points at the day's box.

THE NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Mrs. Canasta lives down one level. Your pills are right in front of you.

Ma gives him a look he has never seen. Empty. Then she snaps back.

MA
Oh, right, I am so sorry. I see them right here. I don't know what got into me.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Wow, she was losing it. I would have to tell Nick. I'm sure he'll get mad like it was my fault.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

A working-class bar. Packers and Bears on a high television. Pool table, dartboard, smoke. The Narrator sits over a beer. Nick comes in, thick, scarred, reddish-brown hair wild, and slaps him on the back hard enough to spill foam.

(SOUND: crowd noise, a cue ball cracking, the television roar)

(MUSIC: bar rock leaking from a corner jukebox)

NICK
Hey, little brother. What's shakin'?

THE NARRATOR
I don't know. Nothin'.

NICK
What's the matter? I can tell you're hidin' something.

The Narrator tells him about Ma. Nick's face goes red.

NICK
I knew she would start going crazy cooped up in that place all day.

NICK (CONT'D)
Why don't you take her to the store or something? Holy Christ, do something with her.

THE NARRATOR
I just needed to get away from her for a while, that's all.

Nick gives him a look like he wants to punch him in the face.

NICK
They take every last dime out of these old people, then leave the family in debt. Is that what you want?

THE NARRATOR
Of course not.

NICK
Then get home. Watch it with her. Get her out tomorrow. At least take a walk.

THE NARRATOR
That's what I'll do.

NICK
That's more like it.

The Narrator pays and stands. Outside thoughts already turning.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Sometimes I really hate Nick inside. He's done so much for us. I would never tell anybody this.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET - NIGHT

Cold enough to sting. The Narrator walks faster than normal. Across the street, a stone library, lights still on.

(SOUND: wind in a bare tree, his shoes on cold sidewalk)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who's gonna know anyway? Ma can barely remember. Nick won't know.

He cuts across.

INT. MILWAUKEE LIBRARY - NIGHT

High windows, wood floors a hundred years old, lights on metal chains over long tables. It smells of old paper. A librarian moves books behind a front desk. The Narrator, jacket still on, looks like he wandered into a church.

(SOUND: a cart wheel squeaking, pages turning)

THE NARRATOR
How do I take a book home from here?

The librarian slides a form across. He writes his name and address. She hands him a temporary paper card.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It was like I could get anything in here for free. Who needed school?

He drifts the stacks and stops in classic literature. Shakespeare, Dickens, Eliot, Dumas, Wilde. He pulls Wuthering Heights because it is there.

At the desk the librarian gives him a thin little smile. He walks back into the cold.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Shit, I missed the whole game. Chicago won by a touchdown anyway.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma already in bed. The Narrator sits under a cheap lamp and opens the book. Heathcliff. Catherine. England a long time ago.

(SOUND: a page turning, the radiator ticking)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
So far it was a little like a soap opera. I got the feeling these two really loved each other.

He stares at the wall.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
This reminded me of Sionna. I needed to call her in the morning.

INT. KIRK STREET BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Narrator asleep. His face twitches.

DISSOLVE TO:

EXT. ENGLISH MOOR - DAY

Dream light, oversaturated. A woman with Sionna's pale face and light-blue eyes wears old riding clothes, hair piled, running happy through heather on a horse. The Narrator is not sure he is in the picture. He only feels it.

(SOUND: hoofbeats on turf, wind across open land)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Somehow I mixed up Sionna and Catherine. I just got this feeling I would end up feeling for her like Heathcliff.

The horse and the woman recede into fog.

INT. KIRK STREET KITCHEN - DAY

Morning. Ma at the table with coffee. They look at each other with nothing to say. The Narrator, same lean face, same short brown hair, tries.

(SOUND: a spoon in a mug, traffic five floors down)

THE NARRATOR
What did you do last night? Why didn't you stay up?

MA
It's so cold outside. I don't have a nice coat to wear. Where are we going anyway?

THE NARRATOR
Just a walk, Ma.

MA
I don't feel like it.

He gives up. He finds Sionna's number. His hands shake.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

The Narrator on the wall phone in the kitchen, cord stretched.

(SOUND: rotary clicks, a faint ring through the receiver)

THE NARRATOR
Hi. Sionna? This is the guy that got your number outside of the pizzeria.

SIONNA (V.O.)
Of course I do. How are you doing?

THE NARRATOR
Fine, ummm, I was wondering if you want to go for that cup of coffee.

SIONNA (V.O.)
Absolutely.

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

College backpacks, textbooks, glasses sliding on and off. The Narrator feels too old the second he walks in. Sionna waits at a small back table, backpack, washed-out jeans, soft leather shoes, long-sleeved loose green hooded top. He does not see the clothes. He sees the watery blue-crayon eyes.

(SOUND: espresso machine hissing, overlapping student talk)

THE NARRATOR
You ever go to school?

SIONNA
I have a degree in English. I love to read and write stories.

He feels like a complete dumbass.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
What are you reading right now?

THE NARRATOR
Wuthering Heights. This guy and girl grew apart but were really in love.

SIONNA
Yea, I know. I read it.

She talks about ghosts, double characters, the moors. Wild, unpredictable, infertile. A love that never changes destroys Heathcliff.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
A love that never changes will destroy you.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It reminded me of Nick. Strong and unwavering. I wondered if it would destroy him too.

Something in him comes awake. They make another date. Dinner. Friday.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET - DAY

The Narrator walks toward a bus stop, stunned and taller somehow.

(SOUND: bus approaching, air brakes)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Maybe that was Ma's problem. Dad left, and Ma never moved on. It just ended up destroying her.

He stops at the curb.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
There ain't no way I could ever forgive him. He is the lowest of the low.

INT. KIRK STREET BEDROOM - NIGHT

The Narrator bolts upright, sweating in a cold room, chest pounding.

(SOUND: his breath tearing, a siren far away)

THE NARRATOR
Hold!

INT. KIRK STREET BEDROOM - NIGHT

He sits on the edge of the bed, same lean body, short brown hair plastered with sweat, staring at his hands as if they still hold a waist.

(SOUND: radiator ticking, a dog barking in the alley)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I hardly ever dreamed anything. I remember it all though.

EXT. ANCIENT FOREST - DAY

Dream. Thick deciduous woods, moss on the north bark of oaks and maples, wood sorrel and trillium underhoof. The Narrator rides close behind a woman on a brown dappled mare. His sleeves are fine white linen, ballooning. A red embroidered tunic. Brown hose. Her long brown hair hangs to the small of her back. He leans to her neck. Rose and warm grass.

(SOUND: hooves in leaf litter, insects, her hair against linen)

They break into an open field. Long grass. He jumps down and lifts her. Heart-shaped fair face. Lightest blue eyes, early morning. First kiss. He surrenders.

The image holds on those eyes.

INT. KIRK STREET BEDROOM - NIGHT

Back on the bed. He presses his palms to his eyes.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
The weird thing was the clothes. It was like it was my life for real. It was Sionna, but in a different place.

He lies back, not sure if he wants to sleep again.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Did I insert these people into Wuthering Heights? It had to be make-believe. Didn't it?

INT. JAVA HUT - DAY

Another afternoon. Same back wall, same watery eyes. Sionna listens to the dream and does not laugh. She smiles through him.

(SOUND: cups set on saucers, a chair scraping)

SIONNA
Some people think dreaming is real, and being awake is not.

THE NARRATOR
I don't get that. I'm sitting in a chair. I'm breathing air. How could all this be just fake?

SIONNA
It's not fake. It just won't last more than the present moment.

She turns her cup.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
Once it was a tree, now it's a chair, someday it will rot and maybe be a tree again.

THE NARRATOR
So what, my dream is a message or something?

SIONNA
Maybe you were dreaming about something from a past life.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
If that was a past life, I wish I could go back there. Especially the girl.

INT. MILWAUKEE LIBRARY - DAY

The Narrator at a table with a Freud book and a slip of paper. He reads, mouthing words.

(SOUND: a copier thumping, someone whispering)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He thought dreams told us what was in our subconscious. If that's true, I want to ride a horse and fall in love. Sounds good to me.

INT. ART MUSEUM GALLERY - DAY

Old paintings, gilt frames. Sionna and the Narrator stand before a self-portrait of a man who looks half-mad. She tilts her head.

(SOUND: their footsteps on stone, a guard's keys)

SIONNA
Intelligence borders insanity. Or something like that.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
A lot of these old painters were crazier than shit. I think I've never known anyone smart enough to be insane.

INT. YOGA STUDIO - DAY

A purple rectangle mat. About twenty people on their backs. The Narrator in a t-shirt and shorts, shirt already trying to fall over his face. Sionna on the next mat, calm, pale, light-blue eyes closed. He tucks the shirt into his waistband so he can see.

(SOUND: an instructor's soft count, bodies shifting on rubber)

He shakes in a down-dog. His groin, his back, his shoulders all argue. Afterward he lies in corpse pose and something unclenches.

SIONNA
You were really great at that.

THE NARRATOR
Are you kidding me?

SIONNA
No. You caught on very quickly. You did like it, didn't you?

THE NARRATOR
Um, yeah. Yeah! I did like it. I just felt so dumb.

SIONNA
That's pretty common. Your practice is your practice. The only thing that matters is what's going on on your mat.

THE NARRATOR
Yeah, well, my practice is not the best practice.

SIONNA
That's why they call it practice.

They both smile.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - NIGHT

Ovens glowing. The Narrator stretches dough and does not hate it. An asshole customer snaps fingers. He just nods.

(SOUND: oven door, a ticket printer, street noise when the door opens)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Making pizzas didn't bother me. Cleaning up didn't bother me. I was just happy to be there. That has never happened, unless you count payday.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Nick stomps, thick and red-faced, reddish-brown hair helter-skelter, pox-marked, fists opening and closing. Ma sits in her chair as if nothing is up. The Narrator tries not to cringe.

(SOUND: Nick's boots, a door still shivering in its frame)

THE NARRATOR
What's going on Nick?

NICK
Ma missed her doctor's appointment yesterday, you ass. Where the hell were you?

THE NARRATOR
I totally forgot. I'm sorry. I was with someone.

NICK
With someone? What the hell is that? With who?

THE NARRATOR
I met a girl, and we had a couple dates. That's all.

Nick stares, black-eyed, fire coming off him.

NICK
Oh, great. A girl. Don't you think Ma deserves better than that?

THE NARRATOR
I said I was sorry!

Nick turns and slams the door in his face.

(SOUND: slam echoing down the hall)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I hardly ever get into it with Nick. You just don't know what he is going to do.

The Narrator looks at Ma. She has not moved.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
One time in high school he sprayed lighter fluid on a kid's yard and set it on fire. Everybody is afraid of Nick.

INT. MILWAUKEE LIBRARY - DAY

Exercise books. Yoga. World religion. The Narrator checks out both. He sits and reads about attachment, suffering, the present moment.

(SOUND: a stapler at the desk, a chair creak)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
It said we look for happiness in the outside world. The more they have, the more they want.

He turns a page.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Life is suffering, and the cause is the search for things. Through meditation we can live only for the present. That was really cool.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

The Narrator on the wall phone. Ma's game show in the other room.

(SOUND: canned television laughter, the phone cord ticking the wall)

THE NARRATOR
I wish you'd finally come over. Meet my Ma.

SIONNA (V.O.)
Are you sure? It's kind of a big deal to meet your mother.

THE NARRATOR
Hell, she probably won't even remember you in the morning.

A beat.

THE NARRATOR (CONT'D)
I read a book about Buddhism. I think I want to try meditating sometime.

SIONNA (V.O.)
You've already tried it, silly. Savasana. Corpse pose. At the end of every class.

He hangs up, a little stupid and a little ready.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Ma in the chair, game show colors on her face. The Narrator sits.

(SOUND: the television, her uneven breath)

THE NARRATOR
How's it goin' Ma?

MA
What? Oh, it's you.

That funny confused look, as if she does not know him.

THE NARRATOR
Are you okay Ma?

MA
Oh, I don't know. It just feels like my heart is going to jump out of my chest.

She swallows.

MA (CONT'D)
I'm a little dizzy too.

He reaches for the phone.

INT. DR. CARSON'S OFFICE - DAY

Grey sky in the window. A siren somewhere. DR. CARSON, middle-aged, serious, white coat, meets the Narrator's eye. Ma is already in another room.

(SOUND: a distant siren, a blood-pressure cuff ripping velcro down the hall)

DR. CARSON
Hello, I'm Dr. Carson. I think I may have met you before. You're the younger son, right?

THE NARRATOR
Yeah, that's right. Uh, how's she doin' Doc?

DR. CARSON
Your mother is fine. She is settled in another room. I want to talk to you about her blood pressure.

THE NARRATOR
She's been takin' her pills. I make sure she takes them religiously.

DR. CARSON
Your mother's blood pressure is dangerously high. I'm afraid of the risk of a stroke.

The doctor does not look away.

DR. CARSON (CONT'D)
Take her directly to the emergency room. She will receive medicine intravenously until we can get her stabilized.

THE NARRATOR
Whoa man. Are you sure it's that bad?

DR. CARSON
She has been confused. And she experienced chest pressure this afternoon?

THE NARRATOR
Yeah. She hasn't been acting too normal lately.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Nick arrives, beet-red, wild hair, already pissed. The Narrator has Ma's coat.

(SOUND: Nick's keys hitting the basket, Ma holding her chest)

NICK
What the hell is this all about now?

MA
Someone must be talking about me.

She hears ringing in her ears. They get her moving.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Three days later. Ma dozes, smaller in the bed. The Narrator has been there almost constantly. SIONNA meets him; they walk the corridor and then outside. Her eyes sparkle like light-blue diamonds.

(SOUND: a monitor beeping softly, cart wheels)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
She made me feel excited and calm all at the same time. I think I loved her. I just hadn't told her yet.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

The Narrator asleep in the chair. Ma dozing. His face twists.

EXT. MEDIEVAL BATTLEFIELD - DAY

Dream. Chaos. Men screaming. Blood on the ground. Shining steel. The Narrator stands over a dying man. Blood bubbles from the mouth.

(SOUND: steel on steel, men screaming, a wet gasp)

The dying man shapes one word.

DYING MAN
Save her.

The Narrator turns. In the middle of dozens of falling men, Nick runs toward him, thick, fire-eyed.

THE NARRATOR
Nick! Nick! Thank God you're here! I thought I was going to die.

NICK
That you are, little brother.

Nick drives the longest knife imaginable into his chest.

(SOUND: a blade punching cloth and bone, the Narrator's gasp)

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - NIGHT

The Narrator wakes gasping, heart pounding. Ma still dozes. It takes him a long time to come back.

(SOUND: the monitor, his ragged breath)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Why would Nick want to kill me? I felt betrayed. That had to be the worst betrayal of all.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Home. A blue plastic weekly pill box on the table, Sunday through Saturday, morning and night. Sionna helped buy it. Ma eyes it like it might bite. The Narrator, lean, tired, shows her.

(SOUND: plastic lid clicking, the television on low)

THE NARRATOR
See Ma, every day of the week. Separations for morning and night.

MA
Oh my Lord, that looks a little overwhelming.

THE NARRATOR
No worries. I'm goin' to be here. Right pill, right time.

MA
You're such a good boy. You and Nick are like a God-send. What would I do without you two?

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
You two? Like Nick was ever around anymore. That was a laugh.

The door opens. Nick, beet-red, hair helter-skelter, pox-marked, prison-break eyes, big boots. He laughs too loud.

NICK
What's up little Bro? You got Ma all tucked in? Man, you shoulda been a nurse!

THE NARRATOR
Knock it off man. Somebody has to make sure she stays on track. Where have you been?

NICK
Don't start giving ME shit Bro. I was at work. More than you can say for that bullshit pizza parlor.

THE NARRATOR
It's eight o'clock. Ma's been home for like four hours.

Nick drops into the brown chair, boots on the stool.

NICK
Are you going to keep her from strokin' out, or what?

The Narrator hands him the blue box.

NICK
Oh, holy shit! Aren't you nurse Nancy! How did you come up with this little idea?

THE NARRATOR
If you must know, Sionna helped me. She's really smart. You know I never could think things through.

NICK
If you must know. This chick sure has you wrapped around her finger.

THE NARRATOR
Stop it Nick! You haven't even met her. Give her a fuckin' break!

Nick stands, a giant red Viking.

NICK
Don't you talk to me like that, you little bastard.

THE NARRATOR
I'm not a bastard!

NICK
How the hell do you know? You sure are a giant pussy. That makes you and me way different Bro.

THE NARRATOR
You're so Goddamn hateful. Was Ma on crack or somethin' when she was pregnant with you?

(SOUND: the slap, a wet crack of palm on skull)

Nick's hand slams across the Narrator's head. The left ear rings. The Narrator goes sick-white.

NICK
Don't you EVER talk that way about MY mother you little fuck.

Nick stomps out. The door slams.

(SOUND: slam, then the apartment's stunned quiet)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had never been in a screaming fight with Nick. It came bubbling out like firecrackers.

He looks at Ma's shut bedroom.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
In that split second, I knew I never wanted Sionna to meet that prick.

EXT. SCHOOLYARD - DAY

Late spring, years earlier. Lilacs at the property line. YOUNG NARRATOR, about nine, ordinary face, short brown hair, folded over laughing until he cannot breathe. A boy his age laughs with him, dribble on his chin. Two girls with braids and red bows shake their heads and walk toward the building.

(SOUND: recess voices, a kickball thud, distant traffic)

YOUNG NARRATOR
You HAVE to learn some responsibility!

The other boy spits it back. They cry it in unison, mocking a teacher.

YOUNG NICK walks up, eleven or twelve, already tall, reddish-brown hair in a devilish mess, the same chin and eyebrow that will scar later.

YOUNG NICK
What are you two numbskulls doin'?

YOUNG NARRATOR
Just the usual crap from Mrs. Stark. She thinks we need to learn some responsibility.

He bursts out laughing again. Young Nick slaps his shoulder.

YOUNG NICK
Little Bro, you better start listening. Ma is pissed already. Don't get sent to the principal.

MRS. STARK strides up, glasses on a beaded necklace resting on her chest.

MRS. STARK
Mr. Olson! Mr. Olson! Would you like to explain yourself before I send you in to Mr. Pauls?

YOUNG NICK
Um, sorry Mrs. Stark. He didn't mean anything. I'll talk to him. Ma will talk to him.

Mrs. Stark looks Young Nick up and down.

MRS. STARK
Well, Nick, I know you're a good student. It's too bad your brother doesn't have the same level head.

She turns on the Young Narrator.

MRS. STARK (CONT'D)
YOU need to follow your brother's example. And Bobby, your mother will be getting a note.

She marches off like a newsreel soldier.

YOUNG NARRATOR
Nick, um thanks man. You saved my skin.

Young Nick smirks a warning and walks toward a knot of sixth-graders who want the story.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
If they only knew the kind of stuff he did. He stole. He was a bully. It was like Nick lived in a bubble.

INT. YOUNG NARRATOR'S BEDROOM - NIGHT

A small boy's room. Young Nick comes in, musses the Young Narrator's hair.

(SOUND: a distant television, a bedspring)

YOUNG NICK
You're such a numbskull, man. I'll always take care of you. Don't forget that.

YOUNG NARRATOR
I won't Nick. I won't.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Present. The Narrator stares out the front window. Grass going green. A slight grin.

(SOUND: oven fans, a ticket printer)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
That must have been what triggered it. He had always been the alpha dog. Sionna hadn't met him yet.

He sighs.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Both of them completed me. He was the leader. She was the soft part. Without both of them, who would I be?

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Months later. Nick in big boots and a brown suede jacket, reddish-brown hair, scars. He kisses Ma, who is watching a cop show. The Narrator stands in the kitchen doorway, leaner from work and school thoughts.

(SOUND: keys in the basket, the cop-show theme)

NICK
Those assholes next to me almost caught me in the goddamn drill press today.

MA
Nick, do you have to swear? It's so unlike you.

NICK
Okay, okay, Ma. Don't want to hurt your pretty ears.

He turns on the Narrator, lips in a strange pursed curl.

NICK
So, what's up with you?

THE NARRATOR
Ah, not much. Just working and thinking about going to school.

Nick stares as if Ma had said she was pregnant.

NICK
Going to school? How the hell are you going to pay for that? And what for?

THE NARRATOR
Community college. Bachelor's in business. Maybe manage a restaurant.

NICK
How the hell will you have time to watch Ma if you're screwing around with those pencil-pushers?

THE NARRATOR
Sionna told me I could qualify for a low-interest student loan. They gave me the money.

NICK
Whoa. You are letting this chick tell you what to do. It sure ain't gonna be me helping when you finish.

THE NARRATOR
I don't want to work in a pizza place my whole life. This was my idea.

He keeps his voice level.

THE NARRATOR (CONT'D)
It would mean a lot if you could just be on my side for once. You are my brother, man.

NICK
Be on your side? How many times have I saved your ass? Can you keep count?

Ma looks up, forehead creased, and silently begs them not to argue. She folds her hands and goes back to the screen.

NICK
Donny Amstel. Locker room. Threatening your bike. I followed him home.

Nick leans in so Ma cannot hear, giggling like a kid.

NICK (CONT'D)
I shoved in his front door. I told him our family had mafia connections. He believed me. What a dumbass.

THE NARRATOR
It did work Nick. You are something else man.

Nick hits his arm lightly.

NICK
You're not just my brother man, you're my best friend.

That catches the Narrator off guard. Then Nick's thin greasy smile.

NICK (CONT'D)
Nothin' is ever gonna come between us. Not school, not Ma, and not this girl. Bro's before ho's.

THE NARRATOR
Sure Nick. Sure. I get you man.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Who the hell is he to call my girlfriend a ho? I just kept it under wraps.

INT. COMMUNITY COLLEGE CLASSROOM - DAY

Middle-aged students, even a grandma. The Narrator is not the oldest. A long essay at the end of the semester. He looks almost hopeful.

(SOUND: chairs, a marker on whiteboard)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
When the cute girls opened their mouths, the stupid shit that came out was amazing. Sionna was a rocket scientist compared to these chicks.

INT. KIRK STREET BEDROOM - NIGHT

A used computer on a desk. Ma in the doorway, confused. The Narrator types.

(SOUND: a beige tower fan, hunt-and-peck keys)

MA
What's going on? What is that thing? Why do you need that?

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I found myself writing a lot more than I needed to. Every day I woke up with a purpose.

EXT. KIRK STREET - DAY

Blue sky, warm. Kids skateboard outside a hardware store. One looks at the Narrator and Sionna and they all laugh. The Narrator bristles. Sionna, brown hair, pale, light-blue eyes, just smiles.

(SOUND: skateboard wheels, the kids' laughter)

THE NARRATOR
What the hell is so funny about us?

SIONNA
Nothing, unless you're thirteen.

THE NARRATOR
I feel like I want to punch that kid. Doesn't that piss you off?

SIONNA
No. Why do you let other people bother you like that? Just ignore them.

THE NARRATOR
Logically I know that. It still pisses me off.

SIONNA
Only when we become masters of ourselves can we really deal with the world.

She laughs about one of those boys becoming Buddha. He laughs too. He leans down and kisses her. Slow motion. No one else matters.

SIONNA
Love is the universal language, and we know how to speak it.

THE NARRATOR
You're the only one I can speak it to, I guess.

SIONNA
We have to learn how to speak it to everyone. Even those kids. Loving kindness.

THE NARRATOR
If my girlfriend is loving everyone, I think I'm going to need a new one.

SIONNA
I could never love anyone like I love you.

THE NARRATOR
I could never love anyone the way I love you either.

They walk. He will remember her face for the rest of his life.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - NIGHT

Werner Street. High twenties ceilings, ornate railings somewhere beyond the mess. Nick on the couch, beet-red, laughing so hard he might fall, smoke sputtering from the corner of his mouth. BOB, flannel, small beer belly, holds a ceramic pipe. SAMMY drinks a Bud, silent extra. The Narrator sits in a rocking chair and smiles like a man among morons.

(SOUND: hard rock from a stereo, a can cracking)

BOB
This girlfriend of mine used to be a freak, but she won't put out anymore.

Nick laughs, reddish-brown hair wild, scars deep in the lamplight.

NICK
Aint' many good looking girls in Milwaukee. Aint' that right Bro?

He points with the smoke.

NICK (CONT'D)
Oh wait. He's got the perfect girlfriend. She has him wrapped right now.

BOB
Are you being pussy whipped? You need a get out of jail free card?

THE NARRATOR
You three wouldn't recognize class and intelligence if it jumped up and bit your ass.

They howl. He leans back.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick pretends to be understanding alone, then calls me out. All that best-friend crap just doesn't hold water.

INT. JOE'S BAR - NIGHT

Saturday packed. Smoke, cue sticks, quarters on the rail, a television no one can hear. AC/DC from the speakers. The Narrator has not been here in six months. It feels dumb. Numb. He orders a Bud beside Nick's Jack and Coke.

(SOUND: roar of talk, bottles, a break on the pool table)

(MUSIC: AC/DC blaring from corner speakers)

A waitress in jeans and a white blouse passes. Nick laughs about Tonya. Then he goes still, looking into the pool room.

NICK
That looks like Lindsey over there. Who's she with? Who's that asshole?

THE NARRATOR
You broke up with her. Drop it man.

NICK
Kiss my ass! Did I ask your opinion?

Nick walks. Bob, Sammy, and the Narrator follow. LINDSEY, pretty, light blonde hair, brown eyes, pink sweater and boots, stands with DARK MAN, black wavy hair, dark eyes, caterpillar brows.

NICK
What's up Lindsey.

LINDSEY
Oh, hi Nick. Not much.

DARK MAN
Who is this?

LINDSEY
Oh, just an old friend.

The room quiets. Pool players lean on cues.

NICK
Where did you meet this one? We keep our ears out.

LINDSEY
Nick, why does it matter to you?

THE NARRATOR
Nick. Leave it alone.

Nick shoves him aside.

DARK MAN
What the fuck is wrong with you? You're in my face like a little bitch!

Nick's nostrils flare. Fists. The Narrator steps closer than he has ever dared.

THE NARRATOR
You don't even know this guy. Lindsey can see whoever she wants. Leave it.

NICK
Keep out of it! You're going to be next if you don't get out of my face asshole.

THE NARRATOR
Stop it! You're crazy as shit! No one cares about your ego Nick!

Time slows. Nick's right fist draws back toward the Narrator's face. Joe and a bouncer rush in. Lindsey yells.

LINDSEY
No! Stop!

Nick head-butts the bouncer and tears free. The Narrator is already running.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET - NIGHT

The Narrator runs like a bear is chasing him, over curbs, around people, lungs burning, nose stinging with cold.

(SOUND: his heartbeat in his ears, shoes slapping pavement)

He is not running home.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

An old duplex living room, green suede sofa, a gray cat later. Sionna, pale, light-blue eyes, opens her arms. The Narrator pours out Sammy, Bob, the fist.

(SOUND: his ragged breathing, a clock in the kitchen)

SIONNA
Your brother tried to hit you.

THE NARRATOR
Yes, he did.

SIONNA
No one does that. No one who really cares about you.

THE NARRATOR
He does care. I don't know if we'll ever talk again.

SIONNA
If he never did, you would be better off. He is a thorn. A controlling ass.

She strokes his face.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
You have been to yoga and meditation half a year. You are becoming a bravely compassionate being.

THE NARRATOR
My purpose....what is my purpose?

SIONNA
I don't know. I suppose we won't know until life is over.

THE NARRATOR
Isn't that a little too late?

He laughs, then stops. She cups his jaw.

SIONNA
You're exhausted. Stay with me tonight.

THE NARRATOR
I have to go check on Ma.

SIONNA
That is exactly where Nick will go. Your Ma is okay for one night. Please stop worrying.

He lies with his head in her lap. She massages his scalp.

THE NARRATOR
The first time I met you, it was like I had finally come home.

SIONNA
Me too honey. Me too.

A watery film on her pale blue eyes.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
The purpose of your life is to find the true self within you, and to honor it.

THE NARRATOR
I hope I can.

His eyes close.

EXT. DREAM LAKE - DAY

Black and white at first. The Narrator in muddy tunic, dirty stockings, folded boots, exhausted. He cups water from the reeds. Wind howls. From the middle of the lake Sionna rises, long brown hair, light-blue eyes, white fabric swirling, dry in the water, arms conducting nature.

(SOUND: wind, water lapping leather, then chains rattling)

Color slams in. Greens, blues, yellows. They embrace. Then Nick emerges from the woods as a giant, teeth huge, eyes of fire, a mace whirling. Footsteps shake the earth.

Sionna spreads her sleeves and flies. An invisible force shoves Nick back. Water against fire. He shrinks and vanishes. She smiles. The Narrator kisses real lips, tastes real hair.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

He bolts upright on the green sofa and nearly slams her jaw. Three a.m. on the kitchen clock.

(SOUND: the couch springs, their disoriented breath)

THE NARRATOR
I had the craziest dream. You were an angel. You protected us from Nick.

SIONNA
Well, that sounds about right.

They shuffle to her bed.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - NIGHT

Months of grind. Ma in her afghan. Sionna reads to her; Ma forgets the story and remembers the feeling. A butternut squash casserole dish in the sink. The Narrator fills the blue pill box with Sionna watching so he will not make a tired mistake.

(SOUND: pages, Ma's little laugh, pills rattling into slots)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick was avoiding us. When Ma asked, I told her poker or the gym. I couldn't bear her thinking badly of him.

INT. KIRK STREET BEDROOM - DAY

The Narrator at the computer, a short paper on Descartes. The doorbell. Sionna in the doorway, hair pinned up, sandals, flowered shirt, exhausted and regal. She says hi to Ma and flops on the bed.

(SOUND: the old buzzer, bedsprings)

SIONNA
Geez, what a day. I have been running around like a chicken with its head cut off.

He watches her mouth move like a vacation.

THE NARRATOR
I love you.

SIONNA
Have you been listening to anything I said?

THE NARRATOR
Every word.

He has. He also has a problem in his jeans. She laughs. He jumps on her. Then:

SIONNA
So, what's your paper about?

THE NARRATOR
Dualism and Descartes. Not a big paper.

SIONNA
Is this bed here when you don't see it?

THE NARRATOR
Does a tree that falls in the forest make a sound if no one hears it?

SIONNA
Do I exist if you don't see me?

THE NARRATOR
Don't fuck with my head. I can't take it anymore.

She cradles his head.

SIONNA
You know my parents were hippies, right? Mom said she got pregnant with me at Woodstock.

THE NARRATOR
I had no idea.

SIONNA
Dad went by Mushroom. Mom went by Jasmine. That's how I got Sionna.

She sits up.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
It's pronounced like Shanna. Matron of the river Shannon. Possessor of wisdom.

THE NARRATOR
Your mom and dad put some thought into that one.

SIONNA
They smoked a lot of pot and dropped a lot of acid. I'm surprised my brain works at all.

THE NARRATOR
Where are they now?

SIONNA
Where all old hippies go. To the farm. Door County. Ten acres, chickens, a cow.

THE NARRATOR
Have you ever done drugs?

SIONNA
That's a loaded question. Why do you ask?

THE NARRATOR
We could hang out at your house and get really stoned and see what happens.

SIONNA
I will do your dirty work. I have study group in a half hour.

He hates the goodbye.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET - NIGHT

Slow night. The Narrator ducks two doors down for a large black coffee. Coming back he nearly walks into KEVIN, gym-built, gelled hair parted crisp, arms like the Hulk.

(SOUND: a diner bell, night traffic)

KEVIN
Hey man, what's up? Haven't seen you much down at the gym.

THE NARRATOR
Just haven't had the time. Classes. Working still.

KEVIN
I heard what happened at Joe's. They said you hauled off and clocked Nick.

Kevin's eyes get big.

KEVIN (CONT'D)
Standin' up to Nick? People at the gym were all talkin'. Nick's little brother is a badass.

THE NARRATOR
It totally isn't the truth. Nick almost kicked my ass. Please tell everybody it wasn't like that.

KEVIN
Sure, sure. But it's all over town.

The Narrator slaps his back and goes, stomach aching.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
If Nick heard this version, he really would kick my ass. I felt like a fugitive.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

Lumpy chocolate couch. A hint of rain. Sionna finds a four-inch blue ceramic pipe in a little red oriental box. She pinches pot from a baggie.

(SOUND: a lighter flint, leaves popping, then coughing)

SIONNA
Wow! That is pretty strong.

He inhales. Flame-thrower lungs. They laugh and cough. Green Day on the stereo.

(MUSIC: Green Day, "Good Riddance," from small speakers)

THE NARRATOR
What do you think this song means?

SIONNA
None of us knows what will happen. We have to make the best of things.

THE NARRATOR
I think I'm stoned. Ha ha ha ha.

SIONNA
I can sometimes see people's auras. You are a beautiful color of blue. Loyalty.

THE NARRATOR
You think so?

SIONNA
You have been loyal to your Ma and your brother your entire life.

EXT. DAIRYLAND ICE CREAM - DAY

Soft-serve on a curved cement bench. Munchies. Then BOB is suddenly there, flannel untucked over the beer belly, Marlboro already coming out.

(SOUND: a freezer compressor, skateboards, a lighter)

BOB
Wow, man! Haven't seen you since the big brawl at Joe's.

THE NARRATOR
Hey man. Bob, this is my girlfriend Sionna.

They shake. Bob chuckles.

BOB
Did you know your boyfriend is famous? He single-handedly conquered the BEAST.

THE NARRATOR
Knock it off Bob.

BOB
You moved like Bruce Lee. Everybody is bad-mouthing Nick. Sayin' he's a pussy.

He nods an apology at Sionna.

THE NARRATOR
Please stop those stupid rumors. Nick is still Nick.

BOB
If you see him, tell him Bob wants to hang. I haven't seen or heard from him since that night.

Bob walks off, flicking ash at traffic. Something nags.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Where was Nick? It was time to end this feud and find him.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - MORNING

Metal burning. The Narrator, hair smashed from sleep, yanks a dry coffeepot off the burner with an oven mitt and throws it in the sink. Ma in the chair starts to cry.

(SOUND: a scorching hiss, then Ma's sobs)

THE NARRATOR
Ma, how many times do I have to tell you NOT to try to cook anything?

MA
I can't remember anything anymore. I'm no good for anything.

THE NARRATOR
You're great Ma. I love you.

MA
Nick doesn't love me. He never comes. Did I do something to make him mad?

THE NARRATOR
I'll go check on him. I'll make sure everything is okay.

MA
My heart is a mess already. Somedays I swear it's going to pound right out of my chest.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY

Ornate old building, twelve-foot ceilings. The door opens on a chain. Nick's crazy brown hair and pox-marked face in the slit.

(SOUND: the chain, then a door creak)

NICK
What do YOU want?

THE NARRATOR
Will you let me in, please? Ma is worried about you.

NICK
Haven't you done enough damage?

The chain comes off. Tornado living room. Papers, trash, food containers, clothes on the rug. The Narrator steps over magazines.

NICK
You've ruined my life! People are talkin' shit. I can't go to the gym.

Nick heats cold coffee in the microwave, a man after revenge.

NICK (CONT'D)
They're sayin' my little brother tried to kick my ass. All because of your big mouth.

THE NARRATOR
Nick, man, whoa. I didn't do anything. I was just concerned you were going to hurt that guy.

NICK
You're not the brother I knew. Some hot shit college kid with a hoity-toity girlfriend.

THE NARRATOR
I'm working my ass off and finding out how stupid I am. That's it, man.

Silence like a verdict. Then Nick stands over him.

NICK
I will let it slide this time. But don't you ever, EVER, interrupt me in public again. You got that?

THE NARRATOR
Yes, Nick. I got that. I will try to rectify the situation.

NICK
I'll come over tomorrow. Tell her I've been sick. She doesn't know about this shit, right?

THE NARRATOR
Of course not. She cried today because she forgot the burner was on.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Ma is laughing. Deep, heartfelt, a sound the Narrator has not heard in years. Sionna, pale, light-blue eyes, listens to farm-outhouse Halloween stories. Peach pie and vanilla ice cream wait.

(SOUND: Ma's laugh, a knife through pie crust)

MA
We were just plain hoodlums! Why don't we go to church anymore?

THE NARRATOR
You always say you don't want to. I'll take you if you want.

MA
Yes, I do. Let's go next Sunday.

The door opens by itself. Nick walks in, thick, scarred, reddish-brown hair. Ma nearly falls out of her chair.

MA
Oh My God! It's you Nick! Where have you been hiding?

NICK
Just busy working and crap like that. This one been treatin' you all right?

Sionna comes from the kitchen with plates, wipes her hands, holds one out.

SIONNA
I'm Sionna. You must be Nick. Nice to meet you, I've heard so much about you.

NICK
Uh, yea, likewise.

SIONNA
You're just in time for pie.

NICK
No thanks. I can't eat that crap. Sugar makes me feel bloated and sick.

He stares her up and down.

NICK (CONT'D)
So, I hear you're a university lady. What are you gonna do when you get done?

SIONNA
College professor, if I can get my student teaching done and write a decent thesis.

NICK
Sometimes all that school just doesn't pay off. It's even harder for a woman.

SIONNA
In the last forty years, bachelor's degrees earned by female students have risen from about 35 to 55 percent.

She sets her plate down, still polite.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
There are more females in college today than males. You can't keep a good woman down.

She takes Ma's plate to the kitchen. Nick sits stunned. No one has ever out-worded him.

NICK
That might be why this country is heading down the tubes. The women can't stay home and take care of their kids.

From the kitchen doorway, calm as a pool:

SIONNA
Women cannot afford to stay at home. Diversity helps us learn. We are all humans trying to make a difference.

She is gone again before he can spit. Ma looks at Sionna like the second coming. Nick's smile goes thin and snake-like.

NICK
Someone in this room has a job tomorrow. Doctor Sionna.

At the threshold he grabs the Narrator's shirt and whispers close.

NICK (CONT'D)
Take care of Ma.

THE NARRATOR
Nick, you know I will.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Later weeks. Nick laying out pills, bringing treats. Ma dotes. The Narrator is out. He keeps his mouth shut through snide remarks.

(SOUND: television, pills, Nick's boots)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Just like A Tale of Two Cities. Best of times, worst of times. If it weren't for Ma, we might not ever see each other.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

A cancelled class, a slow parlor, a free afternoon. Sionna at the computer on creation stories. They talk God, time, energy, prayer.

(SOUND: her keyboard, a cat's bell)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
What if what we believe right now seems ludicrous in another hundred years? Maybe God is just energy.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Saturday. Nick pops up, reddish-brown hair, scars, car keys, and says he will take Ma to St. Patrick's for four o'clock. The Narrator's jaw drops. Ma flutters about what to wear.

(SOUND: Nick's keys, Ma's slippers hurrying)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Nick, at church? You don't just go from scary mother to escorting Mom to mass.

They leave in the burnt-orange Trans Am. He imagines lightning in the aisle.

EXT. WISCONSIN HIGHWAY - DAY

First week of summer break. Sionna's old Toyota Civic hatchback winds eastern Wisconsin. Bright sky, pines, maples. The Narrator in the passenger seat. Postcard country.

(SOUND: tires on sun-soft asphalt, a meadowlark)

THE NARRATOR
They actually like being called Mushroom and Ginger? You're sure?

SIONNA
They wouldn't even respond to Robert and Margery.

THE NARRATOR
So they do have real names.

SIONNA
Mom's parents didn't get tofu. Or wood nymphs. I guess I never got my hopes up unnecessarily.

THE NARRATOR
So your parents are pagans.

SIONNA
Yea. I guess that's as close as we're going to get.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Homework, chips, bed. A normal night. Sionna looks at him with a quizzical nod.

(SOUND: pages, a bag of chips, distant traffic)

SIONNA
Your brother is sure suddenly into taking care of your mother, isn't he?

THE NARRATOR
Does seem kind of weird, doesn't it?

SIONNA
I can only hope I have a son or daughter like you. The circle of life.

THE NARRATOR
Do you want to have children someday?

SIONNA
I'm too busy right now. I'm going on 30 though. I want to have lots of time if I do.

THE NARRATOR
I suppose. I do need someone to change those diapers.

She hits his arm. They mess around and fall asleep. Last normal night.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

September. A feeling of dread on the stairs. The Narrator opens the door on complete quiet. No Ma. No purse. No coat. The answering machine blinks red.

(SOUND: the machine's beep, his own breath)

MACHINE VOICE
You have 5 missed calls.

OFFICER DUPUIS (V.O.)
Mr. Olson, this is Officer Mike Dupuis from the Milwaukee County Sheriff's Department.

A nurse's voice follows, Frodo Hospital, an update on family members. He copies a number with a shaking hand. He calls. He cannot reach Nick. He grabs his bike.

EXT. MILWAUKEE STREET - DAY

He nearly runs a kid off the sidewalk. The kid screams. He does not stop.

(SOUND: bike chain, a skateboard hitting gravel, his panting)

INT. POLICE STATION - DAY

Ancient stone, 1898 plaque, lion heads carved in the window surrounds. A wall of sound. A brunette officer's sorry look. OFFICER DUPUIS, big, graying hair, thin mustache, official.

(SOUND: phones, a holding-cell door, the Narrator's cup trembling)

OFFICER DUPUIS
Thanks for coming down, Mr. Olson. There has been a serious accident.

The Narrator's mouth is felt.

OFFICER DUPUIS (CONT'D)
Your brother, Nicholas Olson, and your mother, Mrs. Olivia Olson.

Dupuis does not look away.

OFFICER DUPUIS (CONT'D)
Last night about 8 p.m. they were hit broadside at Werner and Lowe.

(SOUND: a chair scrape, the cup hitting the desk too hard)

OFFICER DUPUIS (CONT'D)
Your brother is in serious condition at Frodo. Your mother passed away at the scene.

The rest is blur. Water. A phone. A ride.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Scrubs, beeping, urine and chemical smell. Through high glass: Nick's erratic fire-colored hair, a white bandage framing the face he cannot see, wires, machines. A nurse waits.

(SOUND: overlapping monitors, a ventilator hiss)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Punctured right lung. Broken right femur. Rod later. Toxicology pending. They think Nick may have been drunk.

He looks at the nurse.

THE NARRATOR
No. I'm really not up to seeing him. Sorry.

He walks out.

INT. KIRK STREET APARTMENT - DAY

Ma's empty chair. The afghan. The goddamn pills. The Narrator stares, then breaks, a three-year-old bawling, pulling his own hair, water on the floor.

(SOUND: his raw crying, a neighbor's television through the wall)

THE NARRATOR
Die you fucking jackass. Die. Die.

Sionna arrives later and holds him and pats his head.

SIONNA
Just let it out. We'll figure it all out. Don't worry.

INT. FUNERAL HOME - DAY

An old man in a bad suit. Casket catalogs. Pink chiffon. Vaults. Sionna is the voice of reason. Moderate casket. Catholic cemetery. A priest for the mass.

(SOUND: muted organ somewhere, pages of a brochure)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
One day you're talking to her, the next day you're putting her in a box. I would be the only son there.

EXT. CEMETERY - DAY

Few people. Apartment neighbors, old coworkers, Bob solemn. A Rosary Society luncheon already over. Light maple lid. The Narrator throws a handful of dirt. Muted thunk.

(SOUND: dirt on wood, a priest's distant Latin, wind in trees)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I reluctantly threw a handful of dirt. I was really behind on schoolwork and sleep.

INT. HOSPITAL ICU - DAY

Five days after. Nick asking for him. The Narrator comes alone. Nick looks like shit: sallow yellow skin, black and purple bruising going green around the eyes and jaw, oxygen tube, chest tube into a plastic box, right leg a huge limb under the spread, IV on the hand. Crazy hair above the mattress. He flicks off the TV.

(SOUND: the chest-tube drain, a distant paging voice)

NICK
Hey, bro.

THE NARRATOR
Hey.

NICK
I guess I really fucked up, man. Can't believe Ma's gone.

Tears on the bloodshot face. The Narrator spits facts.

THE NARRATOR
They took care of it. The mass was nice. Plot at the top of the hill at St. Mary's.

NICK
I didn't mean to have 3 Jack and Cokes before I remembered church. The cops are saying vehicular manslaughter.

Nick's voice is strangely weak.

NICK (CONT'D)
I was almost killed too. Why would I want to hurt Ma? Like it was all my fault.

THE NARRATOR
Whose fault was it, Nick?

NICK
You think I meant to kill her? You son of a bitch.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
He can look as mean as he wants. He is trapped. He cannot hurt me.

THE NARRATOR
Think about it, Nick.

He turns and leaves Nick's face twisted into rainbows and roadkill.

EXT. CEMETERY - DAY

Late fall sun on a new marker. MOTHER: OLIVIA ANNE OLSON. AUGUST 28, 1933 TO SEPTEMBER 7, 1998. I HAVE FOUGHT THE GOOD FIGHT. Golden mums in a metal vase. The Narrator, lean, wrecked, drips onto new grass.

(SOUND: a plastic bottle pouring, a crow)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Yes, you did, Ma. You did keep the faith. I wonder where dad ever ended up.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - NIGHT

Busy. The Narrator is head pizza maker. TONY, older, no family left here, watches. TOMMY, delivery guy, stares at a map as if Friday is a surprise.

(SOUND: ovens, the phone, Tommy slapping a ticket)

THE NARRATOR
Jesus, Tommy, it's Mr. Jones, 6:00 p.m., Friday, half pepperoni, half mushroom, black olive.

TOMMY
If you're so goddamn smart, you go deliver it.

Tony tosses the Narrator car keys.

TONY
Go on. Get out of here.

EXT. MADISON STREET - NIGHT

Tony's freezing Chevy. The Narrator delivers in under ten and is back in twenty. Three-dollar tip.

(SOUND: a slam of a car door, winter wind)

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - NIGHT

Tony hits his shoulder.

(SOUND: the big ovens, a radio)

TONY
Man, that was quick. How would you like to do more deliveries for me?

THE NARRATOR
Sure, whatever you think.

Tony talks about Ma, about how tired he looks, about help. A substitute son. The Narrator, fatherless, motherless, brotherless, takes it.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

He kisses her and crawls into cold blankets, brain fried, Civil War essay waiting. Sleep hits like a wall.

(SOUND: the old furnace, her later footsteps)

EXT. COUNTRY FARM - DAY

Dream. Chickens, dogs, Sionna's dad blowing pot smoke at a cloudless sky. Her mom loads him with Tupperware, pies, foil casseroles. He drives driveway to driveway. Suddenly Sionna is beside him, extremely pregnant. Then she holds a baby. The newborn has a small pizza where its face should be.

(SOUND: chickens, a screen door, then his gasp)

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

Pitch black. Her body heat. She sleepily asks. He tells her. They laugh.

SIONNA
It means you work too much. It also means you are giving birth to a new idea.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
What if we guaranteed a half hour or they didn't have to pay? Like Domino's. I would talk to Tony tomorrow.

INT. PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Harsh fall, early snow. The Narrator daydreams at the window. BOB walks in, flannel, pupils huge, animated.

(SOUND: the bell over the door, oven roar)

BOB
Little brother. What the hell is going on with Nick? The cops are hounding him. They might lock him up.

THE NARRATOR
He accidentally killed one person.

BOB
He's your brother, man. Nick would fucking kill somebody if they locked him up.

THE NARRATOR
Oh, like he already did? I'm not okay with Nick. I'm not okay with Ma being dead. I'm not okay with anything.

Bob backs up, hands in the air.

BOB
I am so sorry, man. If you need anything, you'll know where to find me.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

She massages his neck. Roses and chocolate cookies on her skin.

(SOUND: her hands on tendon, a timer in the kitchen)

SIONNA
Someone once said not to trip over things behind you. This is your play. I love you.

INT. HOSPITAL ROOM - DAY

Earlier, overlapping time: NICK in the hospital bed, sallow, tubes, fire-colored hair, staring at the ceiling. His V.O. is a different weather.

(SOUND: a nurse's cart, his pulse beep)

NICK (V.O.)
That little bitch. It's all that little bitch's fault.

A doctor comes and goes. Nick's mouth curls.

NICK (V.O.)
What about my pain and suffering? Like it means nothing at all.

INT. LAWYER WAITING ROOM - DAY

No-smoking sign. Nick lights a Marlboro anyway, still thick through the shoulders, scars, reddish-brown hair, but the leg is wrong and the suit will come later. He blows smoke at the ceiling.

(SOUND: a flick of a lighter, a distant copier)

NICK (V.O.)
He had one fucking job. Take care of ma. If she hadn't stepped in, everything would have been okay.

A beehive secretary leads him. A shabby suit waits behind a piled desk. Nick talks. The lawyer's face falls. License revoked. Different lawyer needed. Nick walks out already bled.

INT. COURTROOM - DAY

A fat woman judge, brown hair, big glasses. NICK in a black pinstripe suit, shoes scuffed, palms wet. DANIEL DAWSON beside him, silent, blond going sixty, shined shoes. Drug dealers and punks go first.

(SOUND: a gavel in another case, a bench creak)

NICK (V.O.)
Three kinds of people. Leaders like me. Dipshits like these. Invisible people like my brother.

The judge reads a blood-alcohol number. Nick says yes and no. Involuntary manslaughter. A jury later. Up to ten years, or a year if they are kind.

INT. NICK'S APARTMENT - DAY

Pacing. Smoking one after another. He wants a drink so bad his hands shake.

(SOUND: a lighter, floorboards, traffic on Werner)

NICK (V.O.)
I need to fix the root of the problem. Her. She's going to get a piece of my mind.

INT. CITY BUS - DAY

Route 10. Losers, walkers, green hair, pants at the knees. Nick rides with his bad leg, staring hate at the gray city.

(SOUND: bus brakes, a fare box)

NICK (V.O.)
Shannon? Sarona? Sana? Hippie made-up thing. Stay out of his life.

EXT. MILWAUKEE SIDEWALK - DAY

Memory bleeds over the gray street. A big man in a blue polyester puffy coat and black stocking cap hauls a small boy. YOUNG NICK, about eight, reddish-brown hair, rubber boots skimming cement, hand crushed.

(SOUND: boots on cold sidewalk, a man's heavy breath)

The memory-father stops, bloodshot, spit flying, then shoves the boy up a stairwell. A belt snaps. The boy looks in a dresser mirror at red welts.

NICK (V.O.)
What comes around goes around. It was coming around all right.

EXT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DUSK

Dead plants in pots. Nick knocks hard. Sionna opens a crack, pale, light-blue eyes, hippie sandals, loose clothes, bracelets. A gray cat sulks toward the kitchen. Vegetable-and-bean smell, a flower candle.

(SOUND: hard knocking, then the door chain)

SIONNA
Nick? I never would have expected you here.

NICK
Can I talk to you?

He steps onto the wood. She offers the ratty couch, coffee. He refuses.

NICK
You're the reason I'm probably going to jail. You know that?

SIONNA
What makes you think I'm responsible for you going to jail?

He inches up, Paul Bunyan over her, spit on the last word. She wipes her face. She tries to pass. He walks her into the hallway wall.

NICK
If it wasn't for you messing around with my brother, Ma would be alive. Things would be perfect.

SIONNA
Nick, calm down. This really has nothing to do with me.

NICK
How about paying for my hospital and lawyer bills? You got 20 grand?

He grabs her neck with his left hand. Squeaking. Brown hair in silly-string patterns on the wall. His right hand finds a Swiss Army knife. He flicks it open in front of her face.

(SOUND: a switchblade snicking open, her choked squeak)

NICK
Break it off with him tonight. I'll be back in one week. I better see some fucking cash.

He sets the edge between collarbone and breast.

NICK (CONT'D)
If you leak one word, I have lots of friends. They don't give a shit if your parents are alive or dead.

The front door hits the wall.

THE NARRATOR
Holy shit, Nick, what the fuck are you doing?

The Narrator grabs for the arm. They struggle.

(SOUND: bodies hitting plaster, a wet punch of steel)

The knife hits her hard. Red flies. Weird noises. Nick's right elbow smashes back into his brother's head.

NICK (V.O.)
I got to get out of here.

He wipes the blade on her shirt as she sinks. He walks fast into the dark, blood thickening in his right pocket, left hand paying the bus fare.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - NIGHT

The Narrator against the wall, lowering her. Pale going paler. He rips the flannel, finds the wound near the left shoulder, stacks both hands and smashes down. Sirens coming.

(SOUND: sirens growing, her shallow breath, his wet hands)

THE NARRATOR
Hurry up. Hurry up.

Paramedics take over. A cop pulls him into the front room. Blood on his shirt, hands, pants.

THE NARRATOR
Her name is Sionna. Sionna McAllister. Nick Olson is my brother. He stabbed her and ran.

Beeping on the monitor. A heartbeat. They take her. He does not give a shit about Nick.

INT. LOWELL'S RESTAURANT - DAY

Years later. Seattle. Second-floor window, gray clouds on the Olympics, a ferry on Puget Sound, a cruise ship. THE NARRATOR, early forties now, broader shoulders, cleaner dark coat, Midwest face weathered quieter, stares at a cinnamon roll like a halo. Third coffee.

(SOUND: restaurant murmur, ferry horn far below)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Seven years since my life stopped being what it was. Seven years of hatred for my brother.

The waitress leaves. The roll drips frosting.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
They told me if I hadn't applied pressure she would have died in that hallway. Sionna's savior. Right.

INT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

A Sunday in February after the stabbing, memory. Sionna in a rocking chair, head hanging, blank zombie eyes, none of the old shine. A large foam pillow has lived under her left arm for weeks. The Narrator on the couch.

(SOUND: the rocker, rain on the porch)

THE NARRATOR
Sionna? What's going on? Are you depressed?

SIONNA
I guess I'm a little depressed. Thinking about seeing a therapist.

THE NARRATOR
I feel like you're blaming me. You know it had nothing to do with me, right?

SIONNA
I can't feel me. I can't feel myself anymore.

He squats to her eye line.

THE NARRATOR
You are safe. I will protect you with my life.

SIONNA
As long as there is any doubt, I can't go on with you. Maybe we just need a break.

She looks at her hands.

SIONNA (CONT'D)
Go to Mom and Dad's for a while.

THE NARRATOR
Stay. Stay here with me. Please?

She walks into the bedroom. He leaves for work an hour early, fingers slipping off a cliff.

EXT. SEATTLE STREET - DAY

Pike Place. Tourists, flowers, the original Starbucks line. A keyboard player in a gray felt hat. The Narrator, early forties, dark coat, walks through the Seattle freeze. A girl's dark brown hair flashes. He thinks Sionna and keeps walking.

(SOUND: market crowd, a fishmonger's shout, the keyboard)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I now had my own chain. Tony's Boy's Pizza. I used his name in honor.

INT. SEATTLE YOGA STUDIO - NIGHT

Candles, citrus and lavender. Ten or twelve people. Theme: gratefulness. The Narrator on a mat, older body, still practicing.

(SOUND: a singing bowl, rain on the skylight)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I can't help the void left by Sionna, and the resentfulness toward my own brother. We share DNA. Preposterous.

INT. SEATTLE APARTMENT - NIGHT

Warm. BOZO the cat, orange and white, dismissive on the couch. The Narrator eats, reads, drifts.

(SOUND: rain on the window, the cat's purr)

He dreams a winding dirt road, magnified green hills. An Indian sage in a white wrap walks beside him. A dirty pizza box on the road. The sage is disappointed.

SAGE
Stop judging things. The pizza box is here only when we view it. All things are an illusion. Start living the truth.

The sage is gone. The box is gone. The Narrator wakes smiling, writes it all down beside the cat.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I had to change. I had to find forgiveness.

INT. SEATTLE COFFEE SHOP - DAY

Sun, for once. CHRIS, blonde hair in a sloppy bun, hoop earrings, stained white apron, magnificent. The Narrator finally asks.

(SOUND: steam wand, a grinder)

THE NARRATOR
I don't think I even know your name.

CHRIS
Chris.

They talk about a play on the bulletin board. He asks. She accepts. He books a flight to Milwaukee before he can lose the feeling. Next Thursday, 8:15, one stop in Denver.

EXT. KIRK STREET - DAY

Nothing changed in ten years. Brick fortress, black fire escape, dumpsters instead of dented cans. A young Hispanic couple with a stroller fight the dark-brown door. The Narrator, early forties, broader, stands in the alley that was a playland.

(SOUND: the wooden door, a jet far overhead)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I wanted to cry for all the time I wasted being Nick's little pawn. I was past it. I was a grown man.

EXT. OLD PIZZA PARLOR - DAY

Weeds. The parlor is Central Cosmetology now, pink scissors on the sign, dark inside.

(SOUND: a loose sign chain, empty street)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Tony's son must have sold it. I had to laugh. I thought owning a pizza place would be the top. Now I had twelve.

INT. DELI - DAY

A Reuben, fries, iced tea. Then BOB at the window, greasy burger, hair graying and thinning, thicker waist, deeper lines. He slams the burger down and cackles.

(SOUND: a plate, other patrons turning)

BOB
Holy shit. Is that you, bro? You came back, you son of a bitch.

THE NARRATOR
Bob. What the hell is going on?

BOB
Look at you. Big shoulders. Ivy League talk. You here to visit Nick?

THE NARRATOR
I haven't seen him for 8 years. I need to let the shit go.

BOB
I don't think I could forgive him myself. I used to visit. I couldn't do it no more.

THE NARRATOR
It's not your responsibility. He made his own choices.

BOB
You are a wise man. I took over his apartment. I'm at the factory now.

A bear hug. Bob almost cries.

BOB (CONT'D)
I'm so proud of you, man. You got out of here.

EXT. SIONNA'S DUPLEX - DAY

Smaller, grayer. Naked porch. No chairs, no plants. The Narrator sits in a rental Camry like a stalker. He remembers bamboo rockers, a cat named Smiley, an air kiss.

(SOUND: kids on bikes, a German shepherd's nails)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
I loved her so much. I just want her to be happy. I am doing the right thing by leaving her alone.

He looks in the rearview.

THE NARRATOR
Well, you can't look back, man, because you are moving forward.

EXT. GREEN BAY PRISON - DAY

Oppressive brick. Gravel lot. Cooler air. The Narrator walks toward metal detectors and burly men.

(SOUND: wind on chain-link, a distant slam)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
No arguing. No blaming. No judgment. I have evolved even if he hasn't.

INT. PRISON VISITING ROOM - DAY

Plexiglass. Phones. A woman two cubicles down. The Narrator waits. Then NICK comes: older, scrawny beside his former self, hair graying at the sides and thinning on top, lined forehead, watery sad bags under the eyes, the same scars on chin and eyebrow like fossils.

(SOUND: doors locking and unlocking, amplified voices, the phone clicking)

NICK
Hi, bro. Glad you came.

THE NARRATOR
Hi, Nick. Good to see you finally. How are you holding up?

NICK
Not a cakewalk. Looking pretty good for getting out early. Parole hearing in 2 years.

THE NARRATOR
Well, that's good for you, Nick.

NICK
Yeah. Good for me, I guess.

THE NARRATOR
I would appreciate it if you would just listen to me. Okay?

NICK
Yeah. Okay, man. Okay.

THE NARRATOR
I am no longer interested in seeing you. You're an asshole that killed my mother.

Nick looks at the Formica.

THE NARRATOR (CONT'D)
You're an asshole that held me down so you could have the big money.

A breath.

THE NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Mostly, you're an asshole for trying to kill the love of my life.

Nick's eyes fill.

THE NARRATOR (CONT'D)
Now that you know, I have to add this. I forgive you. I forgive you because it sets me free.

The tension of thirty years lets go. He is almost smiling.

NICK
I'm so sorry. I really didn't expect this. You're so right. I am a huge asshole.

Tears plap on the gray surface.

NICK (CONT'D)
I feel so bad about Ma and Sionna. You're more than I ever was. I deserve to live in here.

A guard signals time.

THE NARRATOR
I still believe in you. I still love you for being my brother. Do you hear me?

NICK
I hear you, man. I love you too. What are you doing now?

THE NARRATOR
I own a pizza chain in Seattle. You always said I couldn't do it. I did it, Nick.

NICK
President Olson. That's fucking amazing. I am so proud of you, man. I love you.

The far guard moves him. Nick smiles.

NICK
Bye.

The Narrator hangs up and watches him shuffled down the hall. He sits with the yellow receiver, exonerated.

EXT. WISCONSIN HIGHWAY - DAY

Rolling hills past the rental car. The weight of thirty years gone.

(SOUND: tires, a soft radio, his easy breath)

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
Somewhere, she's out there. She's happy. I have to believe that. I hope she believes that I'm happy too.

INT. MILWAUKEE AIRPORT - DAY

A boarding queue. Mixed nuts and tea. The Narrator, early forties, broader, dark coat, floats above the throng. Nick's teary voice still in his head. An attendant scans the pass. He looks down at the name.

(SOUND: the scanner beep, gate chatter, a distant jet)

NICK (V.O.)
President Olson.

THE NARRATOR
That's me. President Peter Olson.

He whispers it to himself, almost smiling, and walks down the jetway.

THE NARRATOR (V.O.)
This has been Nick and me.

The jetway mouth frames him small against the bright oval of the plane door, then takes him.

> FADE OUT.

THE END
